Z||000000||MooCow||08-02-2000||09:56 PM||||This is making so made that at first I was just gonna sell the thing but then I sat in it and just couldn't do it.  The peoble that I have is every now and again the power windows won't go up. If I jiggle the door then poof it works.  Sometimes the power trunk release won't work so I take it out and jiggle the wires and poof it works for a while. Also I now have to wait 5-8 seconds before the car will even start up.  Could my radio deck take that much power? As soon as my deck flips over tehn it works most of the time.  Sometimes I have to wait almost hours. Can I rewire all these things gone bad.  Like just follow all the power window wires and replace them with new ones or not??<BR>Will new spark plugs and wires help it start up?? Sorry this is so long but I really don't want to give this baby up when I have only had her for 17 days now. All info will help me out greatly.<BR>Also what does the starter look like and were at on an 86 SE v6 4 speed????<BR>                                Thanks Ken||||reg||8
Z||000001||Carrolles||08-03-2000||09:51 AM||||The starter is on the front side of the engine at the bottom on the driver's side.  You'll have to get under the car to see it or do any maintenance on it.<P>It sounds like the wiring connectors for the troublesome components have some corrosion.  You might try uncoupling each connector and cleaning the contacts.<P>Electrical gremlins are a pain in the rump.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000002||Frank R||08-03-2000||12:03 PM||||Electrical problems can be very difficult.  Most problems can be solved by very carefully checking all the ground connections.  In checking the ground connections, start the search with the largest ground, the battery connections.  Every black wire used on GM cars is a ground.  On Fiero cars, there are many ground connections that are out of sight and require some diligent effort.  To thoughly check every ground requires the use of a wiring diagram with akk the grounding places and carefully marking each place as connections are cleaned, checked and tightened.  For some convenience, I have relocated some connections.  On a v6 the grounds located on the front of the engine are difficult to see and retighten.  Window failure is most always a poor ground connection.     FrankR ||||reg||6
Z||000003||rogergarrison||08-03-2000||08:34 PM||||seems awful weird that your having so many different but elec related problems. Try the simple first. make sure your battery cables and connection at battery are clean and tight.Then go ahead and check the component connectors. If thats not it, cross your fingers and hope you didnt buy a flood damage car. If it is from a flood your nitemare has just begun.||||reg||1
